  "core_thesis": "When a company deploys a system whose decisions cannot be explained, it has manufactured a product that is structurally immune to accountability — and that immunity is the defect.",
  "argument_structure": [
    {
      "point": "Opacity is a design choice, not a law of physics — and the choice to deploy an unexplainable system in a consequential context fails any honest risk-utility analysis.",
      "evidence": "Cynthia Rudin's research showing interpretable models match black-box performance in criminal justice and healthcare; the Restatement (Third) of Torts risk-utility framework for design defects; the fact that developers choose neural network architectures over interpretable alternatives for speed-to-market and competitive advantage, not because no alternative exists. When an interpretable model exists and you ship the opaque one anyway, you've made a design choice — and that choice has a body count in denied loans, wrongful fraud accusations, and misdiagnoses.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-intentions-040"
    },
    {
      "point": "Right now, when an opaque AI system harms someone, the victim cannot prove what went wrong — and the developer has no legal obligation to show them. The information asymmetry is total.",
      "evidence": "Michigan's MiDAS system falsely accused 40,000+ people of unemployment fraud with a 93% error rate and no explanation pathway. The Dutch SyRI scandal destroyed families with opaque fraud scores. In Loomis v. Wisconsin, the court said a defendant couldn't even see the algorithm sentencing him — and ruled that was fine. ECOA and FCRA require adverse action notices for credit decisions, but lenders using ML models routinely struggle to produce legally compliant explanations. The FTC has acknowledged the problem but enforcement is sporadic. The affected person is left holding the bag.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-029"
    },
    {
      "point": "Without a design defect standard, the industry will substitute performative compliance for genuine accountability — model cards and bias audits that document nothing about why a specific person was denied.",
      "evidence": "Raji et al. (2020) and Mitchell et al. (2019) show model cards and datasheets are frequently incomplete, inconsistent, or uninformative about actual decision logic. The EU AI Act's transparency requirements and NIST AI RMF both acknowledge documentation alone is insufficient. Companies already treat compliance as a checkbox exercise — publish the model card, run the audit, ship the product. A prima facie design defect standard forces the question upstream: if you cannot explain the decision, you cannot demonstrate it was lawful. The burden belongs on the entity that made the design choice, not on the person who got denied.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-intentions-017"
    },
    {
      "point": "The prima facie standard is moderate, not radical — it creates a rebuttable presumption, not a ban. The developer can justify opacity. But they have to justify it, not hide behind it.",
      "evidence": "Products liability law already works this way for pharmaceuticals, medical devices, and automotive components — the manufacturer must demonstrate the design's benefits outweigh its risks. FDA requires medical device manufacturers to validate and explain algorithmic decision-making under 21 CFR Part 820. The prima facie framing simply extends this existing logic to AI: if your product makes consequential decisions about people and you cannot explain those decisions, the law presumes that's a defect unless you prove otherwise. Low-stakes applications rebut easily. High-stakes ones face the bar they should face.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-102"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
    {
      "frame": "This is a products liability question, not a technology policy question — treat AI like any other manufactured product that can injure people.",
      "why": "This framing strips away the tech-exceptionalism that lets AI developers avoid the accountability every other manufacturer faces. A car manufacturer cannot ship a vehicle with no brakes and call it an 'inherent technical limitation.' A pharmaceutical company cannot sell a drug with unknown side effects and call it 'complexity.' The framing forces opponents to explain why software gets a special exemption from the rules that apply to every other product on the market."
    },
    {
      "frame": "The question is not whether AI should be explainable — it's who pays when it isn't. Right now, the answer is: the person who got denied, fired, or accused.",
      "why": "This framing makes the cost distribution visible and visceral. It prevents the debate from becoming an abstract technical discussion about XAI methods and keeps it anchored to the material reality: someone is harmed, they cannot find out why, and the company that built the system has no obligation to tell them. The design defect standard simply moves the cost to the entity that created the risk."
    }
  ],
  "anticipated_challenges": [
    "Opponents will argue that explainability is technically infeasible for frontier models — I need to reframe this as proving my point: if you cannot explain it, deploying it in consequential contexts IS the defect.",
    "Opponents will argue this standard will chill innovation and force companies to use inferior models — I need the Rudin research and the risk-utility framework ready to show that 'inferior' is often a marginal accuracy difference that does not justify total accountability immunity.",
    "Opponents will challenge the analogy to traditional products liability by arguing software is fundamentally different from physical products — I need the medical device regulation precedent (FDA already treats algorithmic medical devices as products subject to design defect analysis) to show this line has already been crossed.",
    "Opponents may argue the deployer, not the developer, should bear responsibility for context-inappropriate use — I need the component part manufacturer doctrine and the argument that the developer controls architecture and could have built explanation capacity in.",
    "Key assumption I'm exposing: that interpretable alternatives exist or are feasible for most high-stakes domains. If opponents can show domains where no interpretable model comes close to adequate performance AND the stakes of not deploying are high (e.g., certain medical diagnostics), I need to concede that case specifically while holding the general principle.",
    "Key assumption I'm exposing: that the legal system can coherently define 'sufficient explanation.' I need the functional standard ready — can the affected person understand why and identify what to change — rather than getting dragged into a technical debate about SHAP values vs. counterfactuals."
  ]
}
